    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> was a slave society from the outset: about 35 per cent of all Africans taken in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> were sent there, more than ever reached the Thirteen Colonies, and the trade lasted longer than in any other country of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> made the colony: from 1600 to 1650 it was 95 per cent of exports, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> sugar regions owners found it cheaper to work the enslaved to death over a few years and buy replacements.</li><li>The enslaved fled to fortified settlements called quilombos; the largest, the Quilombo dos Palmares under Ganga Zumba and Zumbi, grew to many thousands and held out until Domingos Jorge Velho destroyed it and killed Zumbi in 1695.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Only two of the fifteen hereditary captaincies into which John III divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1534–1536 prospered: Pernambuco by growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, and São Vicente chiefly by capturing Indigenous people to trade them as slaves.</p></aside>
    </section>

