    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral.jpg" alt="Pedro Álvares Cabral" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_%C3%81lvares_Cabral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aurélio de Figueiredo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1494 Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> divided the New World along the Tordesillas meridian, and on 22 April 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a>, leading 13 ships and more than 1,000 men on the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, landed at what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> Seguro and took possession of the land for King Manuel I. For thirty years the Portuguese, busy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and India, sent few expeditions, mostly to cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilwood">brazilwood</a> for red dye with the labour of Indigenous people paid in mirrors, knives and axes. French competition for the wood brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martim-afonso-de-sousa">Martim Afonso de Sousa</a>'s expedition of 1530 to patrol the coast and found the first villages, among them São Vicente.</p><p>Between 1534 and 1536 John III granted fifteen hereditary captaincies to merchants, soldiers and petty nobles. Only two prospered: Pernambuco, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duarte-coelho">Duarte Coelho</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda</a> in 1536 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> mills followed from 1542, and São Vicente, which lived chiefly by enslaving Indigenous people; the rest failed through Indigenous resistance, shipwrecks and quarrels. In 1549 the king made colonisation a royal enterprise, sending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-de-sousa">Tomé de Sousa</a> as first governor-general with the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-da-nobrega">Manuel da Nóbrega</a>. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> on All Saints Bay, divided into an upper administrative town and a lower harbour, and the diocese followed in 1551. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mem-de-sa">Mem de Sá</a> (1557–1573) defeated the Indigenous peoples around Salvador and, with the Jesuits, expelled the French from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> Antarctique; his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estacio-de-sa">Estácio de Sá</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1565.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ouro-preto.jpg" alt="Ouro Preto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Ouro Preto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conjunto_arquitet%C3%B4nico_e_urban%C3%ADstico_de_Ouro_Preto.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Raquel Mendes Silva, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s economy ran in export cycles: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilwood">brazilwood</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, then gold and diamonds. Sugar, planted from the mid sixteenth century, was by far the chief product for 150 years and made up 95 per cent of exports between 1600 and 1650. Trade was restricted to Portugal, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> Brazilian traders exchanged cachaça and shells for people. At the end of the seventeenth century the bandeirantes found gold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, and in the first half of the eighteenth century a rush of people from the colony and from Portugal built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Vila Rica de Ouro Preto</a>, Sabará and Mariana from 1711. The Crown took a fifth, the quinto, cast at the Casas de Fundição from 1725.</p><p>All of it rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. After a brief use of Indigenous labour to cut brazilwood, the Portuguese enslaved Indigenous people for the cane fields, continuing after Sebastian I's law of 1570 freed them through the loophole of "just wars": as many as 40,000 may have been taken from the interior by 1580. Enslaved Africans, familiar with agriculture and more resistant to Old World disease, cost more and became the main workforce; about 35 per cent of all Africans captured in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a> were sent to Brazil. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> sugar districts it was often cheaper to work people to death over a few years and replace them. In Minas Gerais enslaved and freed Black people made up three quarters of the population.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/olinda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olinda.jpg" alt="Olinda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Convento_de_S%C3%A3o_Francisco_-_Olinda_-_Pernambuco_-_Brasil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Valdiney Pimenta, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The colony's wars were against Indigenous peoples, foreign rivals and the enslaved who fled. Duarte da Costa's governorship (1553–1557) was consumed by wars around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>, and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, Pero Fernandes Sardinha, was killed and eaten by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caete">Caeté</a> after a shipwreck in 1556. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mem-de-sa">Mem de Sá</a> forced the submission of the peoples near Salvador from 1557 and in 1560 attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> Antarctique; Nóbrega and Joseph of Anchieta pacified the Tamoio who supported the French, and the last were expelled in 1567. During the Iberian Union <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> privateers sacked Salvador in 1604 and took it in May 1624 for eleven months; from 1630 to 1654 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda</a> and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraiba">Paraíba</a> fell in 1635, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> coast as Dutch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> until it withdrew in 1654.</p><p>Inland, the bandeiras of the Paulistas marched for months to enslave Indigenous people and find minerals. Against the quilombos the colonists sent soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> to destroy the settlements and kill or re-enslave their people, so the quilombos were heavily fortified with dikes, false roads, covered traps and sharpened stakes in the African manner; Palmares, which grew to many thousands during the Dutch wars, resisted Dutch and Portuguese attacks until Domingos Jorge Velho's army destroyed it and killed Zumbi in 1695. In the south Colónia do Sacramento, founded in 1679, was fought over repeatedly, and the Guaraní War of 1756 destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal.jpg" alt="Marquis of Pombal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armas_marqueses_pombal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jsobral, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> was governed first through private captaincies, then from 1549 by a royal governor-general at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> with detailed instructions from the king. In 1621 Philip II made it the State of Brazil and cut off a separate State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-luis">São Luís</a>. Governors of high nobility used the title of viceroy from about 1640, and in 1763 the capital moved from Salvador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, nearer the mines. Each town had a câmara municipal of landowners, merchants and slave traders regulating commerce, infrastructure, artisans and prisons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-christians">New Christians</a>, converted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> prominent as merchants, held civil and church offices, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> was never permanently established.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> were the spiritual arm of the enterprise. Supported by the king, Nóbrega, Anchieta and their companions founded missions at Salvador and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> and gathered Indigenous people into aldeias where they worked and were evangelised, a policy that after 1557 accepted force; epidemics, forced removals and colonists' raids for labourers made the aldeias places people fled. The Jesuits fought colonists who wanted Indigenous slaves and quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Sardinha, but seldom objected to the enslavement of Africans. In 1755 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a> abolished the enslavement of Indigenous people and the legal discrimination against Europeans who married them. The Crown's mining law was hated: the quinto fed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Vila Rica</a> revolt of 1720 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> conspiracy of 1789.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-language"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portuguese-language.jpg" alt="Portuguese language" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-language">Portuguese language</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Breve_discurso_em_que_se_contem_a_conquista_do_Reyno_de_Pegu_na_%C3%8Dndia_Oriental,_pelos_portuguezes_em_tempo_do_Viso-Rey_Ayres_de_Saldanha;_sendo_capitam_Salvador_Ribeiro_de_Souza,_chamado_Massinga,_natural_de_Guimaraens._Rei_do_Pegu.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Victorcouto, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> gave Portugal its wealth and its name to a continent-sized country. Brazilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> undercut it, and then Minas gold, which paid for textiles and weapons from industrial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, carried the Portuguese economy. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>, which broke into many republics, Brazil stayed one unit, united by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-language">Portuguese language</a> and Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, and became the largest country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. The bandeirantes pushed its frontiers far west of the Tordesillas line, confirmed by the Treaty of Madrid of 1750, which with Tordesillas reflects the present extent of its coastline.</p><p>The colony remade peoples and landscapes. On the caboclo frontier Indigenous, European and African people exchanged beliefs, music, remedies and techniques; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupi">Tupi</a> gave Portuguese its words for plants, animals and places, and Kimbundu gave it fubá; runaways taught Indigenous people Portuguese, so that one frontier agent judged a fugitive Black man could do more among the Indians than all the missionaries. The Black Irmandade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> built a culture of festivals and elaborate burials from African and Christian elements, and some quilombos survive as rural communities. The colonists' view of nature as disposable denuded 4,000 square kilometres of Atlantic Forest for mining, and their slash-and-burn fields, surrendered to leaf-cutting ants, and untended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> turned native grassland into degraded, scrubby pasture.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil.jpg" alt="Pedro I of Brazil" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Pedro I of Brazil</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DpedroI-brasil-full_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Simplício Rodrigues de Sá, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The colonial order wore out from its own cycles. Gold production declined towards the end of the eighteenth century, leaving the hinterland stagnant, and the Crown's intention to enforce payment of all debts, the derrama, drove the white upper class of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inconfidencia-mineira">Inconfidência Mineira</a> of 1788–89: a republic with an elected leader, but with property and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> intact. Discovered in 1789 before the rising, it ended with eleven exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiradentes">Joaquim José da Silva Xavier</a>, Tiradentes, hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1792; he became the symbol of Brazilian independence.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> ended the colony. When French troops invaded Portugal in 1807, Prince Regent John moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>; in January 1808 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> he opened Brazil's trade to friendly nations, breaking the colonial pact that had tied it to Portugal alone, and in March reached Rio de Janeiro, where he founded medical schools, military academies and the first bank of Brazil. In 1815, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, he raised Brazil to a kingdom within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</a>, the formal end of the colonial era; the captaincies became provinces. Called back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1820">Liberal Revolution of 1820</a>, John VI left his heir, who refused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes">Cortes</a>' demand to reduce Brazil to a colony on the Dia do Fico, 9 January 1822, and was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a> after independence on 7 September 1822.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, Portugal's possession from Cabral's landing in 1500, passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilwood">brazilwood</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> to the gold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> on the labour of enslaved Africans and Indigenous people, who resisted in quilombos such as Palmares; governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, it ceased to be a colony when Prince Regent John raised it to a kingdom in 1815.</em></p>

